---
import Developers from "~/layouts/Developers.astro";
import { GROVE, INSTALL } from "~/data/developers";

const seedlingInstall = `URL=https://github.com/brig-id/server-seedling/releases/latest/download
FILE=seedling-linux-$(uname -m)
curl -fsSLO "$URL/$FILE" && curl -fsSL "$URL/SHA256SUMS" | grep " $FILE\\$" | sha256sum --check
sudo install -m 755 "$FILE" /usr/local/bin/seedling
sudo seedling init --domain id.example.org --systemd   # configuration, key, service
sudo systemd-sysusers && sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now seedling`;

const groveInstall = `git clone https://github.com/brig-id/server-grove && cd server-grove
cp deploy/grove.env.example deploy/grove.env    # domain, image, bucket of the replica
# the four secrets: see the top of deploy/compose.yaml
docker compose -f deploy/compose.yaml --env-file deploy/grove.env up -d`;

const commands = [
  [
    "signup [open | invitation | closed]",
    "Who may create an account. By invitation until changed.",
  ],
  [
    "admin link",
    "A link that makes an administrator: the first one, or again after every administrator lost their passkeys.",
  ],
  [
    "client policy [open | listed | registered]",
    "Which websites that describe themselves are accepted. open by default.",
  ],
  [
    "client accept | refuse | unlist <url>",
    "The two lists, by the address of a website's document.",
  ],
  [
    "client limit --allow <attribute> … | --all | --none",
    "What such websites may ask for, at most (in the next release).",
  ],
  [
    "client add --name … --redirect-uri … [--public]",
    "Register a website of your own, or one under development on your machine (http on localhost).",
  ],
  [
    "client list | attributes | remove",
    "The applications: registered, and those that describe themselves.",
  ],
  [
    "rotate-key --old … --new …",
    "Re-encrypt everything under a new master key.",
  ],
  [
    "rotate-oidc-key",
    "A new key to sign ID tokens; the old one keeps verifying for a while.",
  ],
  ["--version", "What runs. The About page of the server says it too."],
];

const settings = [
  [
    "BRIGID_SERVER__DOMAIN",
    "The domain of the server. It cannot change later: passkeys are bound to it.",
  ],
  ["BRIGID_SERVER__PUBLIC_URL", "Its address as people reach it, https."],
  ["BRIGID_DATABASE__PATH", "The SQLite database."],
  [
    "BRIGID_MASTER_KEY_FILE",
    "The file holding the master key (a Docker secret, a systemd credential).",
  ],
  [
    "BRIGID_SECURITY__TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR",
    "true only behind a reverse proxy that replaces X-Forwarded-For.",
  ],
  ["BRIGID_SERVER__METRICS_PORT", "Prometheus metrics, on an internal port."],
];
---

<Developers
  title="Run a server · Developers"
  description="Run a brig·id server: seedling on one machine, grove in a container with its database replicated. Installation, settings, operator commands."
  lead="Run a server,"
  accent="yours."
  intro="Two editions run today, on the same engine: seedling, one file on one machine, for you and yours; grove, a container with its database copied elsewhere as it changes, for a community. What an account or an identity is does not depend on the edition."
>
  <div class="prose rise">
    <h2>seedling: one file</h2>
    <p>
      A static binary for any Linux, x86_64 or aarch64, with the interface
      inside. It links nothing and needs nothing installed beside it.
      <code>seedling init</code> writes the configuration, makes the master key
      and installs the systemd service.
    </p>
  </div>
  <pre class="code rise"><code>{seedlingInstall}</code></pre>
  <div class="prose rise">
    <p>
      <a href={INSTALL}>The install guide</a> takes it step by step, from the
      download to a first sign-in: the certificate, the first administrator,
      connecting a website, updating, backing up.
    </p>

    <h2>grove: a container, replicated</h2>
    <p>
      The same server as an image (<code>ghcr.io/brig-id/grove</code>), with a
      compose file that runs it beside Litestream: every change of the database
      reaches an S3-compatible bucket within a minute (Cloudflare R2, Backblaze
      B2, MinIO), encrypted with a key the bucket never keeps, with a week of
      history. A reverse proxy in front holds the certificate. grove is
      configured by its environment alone.
    </p>
  </div>
  <pre class="code rise"><code>{groveInstall}</code></pre>
  <div class="prose rise">
    <p>
      <a href={GROVE}>Its repository</a> holds the compose file and what each
      secret is. The project's own server, helm.brig.id, runs grove.
    </p>

    <h2>The operator's commands</h2>
    <p>
      The same for both editions: <code>seedling …</code> on a machine,
      <code>docker compose exec grove /grove …</code> in a container. They work
      on the database, with the server running or not, and need the master key
      like the server.
    </p>
  </div>
  <table class="ref rise">
    <tbody>
      {commands.map(([command, text]) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <code>{command}</code>
          </th>
          <td>{text}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="prose rise">
    <p>
      An administrator does most of it from the interface too: accounts,
      applications, which websites are accepted, sign-up and invitations, and a
      log of every change. An administrator sees that accounts exist, never what
      they hold.
    </p>

    <h2>Settings</h2>
    <p>
      Every setting is an environment variable, <code>BRIGID_</code> then the
      section and the name; seedling also reads them from its configuration
      file. The ones that matter most:
    </p>
  </div>
  <table class="ref rise">
    <tbody>
      {settings.map(([name, text]) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            <code>{name}</code>
          </th>
          <td>{text}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="prose rise">
    <h2>Keep it running</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The master key is the one thing to keep offline.</strong>{" "}
        Without it the database, and grove's replica, cannot be read; nobody can
        give it back.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>GET /health</code> answers 200 while the server works; metrics are
        on their internal port.
      </li>
      <li>
        A new release is a new file (seedling) or a new image (grove): the
        database is upgraded in place at start.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="next rise">
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/editions/">
      Which edition
    </a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/developers/trust/">
      Security and trust
    </a>
  </div>
</Developers>
